\begin{remark}
\rm 
To prove Theorem~\ref{THM:main}, we first develop a novel approach to establish
the LDP for the linear Schr\"odinger equation under the sole assumption that
the initial data lies in $\mathcal{F} L^1$. We then carry out the nonlinear analysis,
together with a perturbative argument, entirely within the $\mathcal{F} L^1$ framework.
\end{remark}

As a consequence of Theorem \ref{THM:main} and the observation \eqref{obser}, we can improve \eqref{THM_LDP3} in terms of the decay of $c_k$.

\begin{corollary}\label{COR:main} 
Let $u(t,x)$ be solutions to \eqref{weakNLS1} with initial data $u_0^\omega$ given in \eqref{random_data}.
For any $t = \mathcal O ( \varepsilon^{-1} |\log \varepsilon|)$ and $z_0 > 0$, we have that \eqref{THM_LDP3} holds provided $(c_k) \in \ell^1$.
\end{corollary} 

\begin{remark}
\rm 
We note that Corollary \ref{COR:main} improves \cite[Theorem 1.1]{GGKS21} in the following sense.
The coefficients $(c_k)$ in \cite[Theorem 1.1]{GGKS21} is required to decay condition \eqref{decay}.
In Proposition \ref{PROP:linear2}, it requires only $(c_k) \in \ell^1$.
It is expected that \eqref{THM_LDP3} holds for initial data $u_0 \in L^2 \cap L^\infty$, where $\mathcal{F} L^1 \subset L^2 \cap L^\infty$.
However, our current argument can only handle $\mathcal{F} L^1$ data.
\end{remark}

