After stating Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, the authors explain that earlier proofs (Cheng--Luo--Malchiodi--Ruiz, \emph{CLMR23}; Frank--Viaclovsky, \emph{FV23}) use “a generalization of the Jerison-Lee's differiential identity combined with integral estimates,” whereas they “take a completely different approach,” modeled on the Euclidean “method of moving spheres.”

Their scheme for proving the main theorem is:
\begin{itemize}
\item Establish Heisenberg-group analogues of the Li–Nirenberg–Zhu calculus lemmas, based on a suitable notion of inversion: define the “generalized CR inversion” $\Phi_{\xi,\lambda}^\beta$ (Definition~\ref{def:gndcrinv}), justified by Proposition~\ref{prop:justifydefn}, which shows an invariance property like \eqref{eqn:funceqn}.
\item With “these two calculus Lemmas of Li-Nirenberg-Zhu type in hand, combined with a Terracini-type integral inequality,” run the moving spheres argument “following the same scheme as was used by Li and Zhang in Section 2 of Li--Zhang, \emph{LZ03} (also in Li--Zhang, \emph{LZ95}).”
\item Concretely, there are “three main steps”:
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item “initiate the moving spheres procedure starting from small radii;”
  \item “show that if the procedure stops, the solution must coincide with its generalized Kelvin transform;”
  \item “apply our calculus lemmas to demonstrate that the solution necessarily takes the Jerison–Lee profile.”
  \end{enumerate}
\end{itemize}
They refer later for the complete argument.

