\label{s:intro}

We look at the Cauchy problem for the doubly degenerate weighted parabolic
equation
\begin{alignat}{2}
  \label{eq:pde}
  f(x)
  \pder{u}{t}
  -
  \Div\big(
  f(x)
  u^{m-1}
  \lvert\operatorname{\nabla} u\rvert^{p-2}
  \operatorname{\nabla} u
  \big)
  &=
    0
    \,,
  &\qquad&
           \text{in $S_{T}$,}
  \\
  \label{eq:initd}
  u(x,0)
  &=
    u_{0}(x)
    \,,
  &\qquad&
           x\in\R^{\SpDim}
           \,.
\end{alignat}
Here $S_{T}=\R^{\SpDim}\times (0,T)$, $0<T\le +\infty$, $x=(x_{1},\dots,x_{N})$, $\operatorname{\nabla} u$ [respectively, $\Div$] is the gradient [respectively, the divergence] with respect to $x$, and we denote
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:expweight}
  f(x)
  =
  e^{g(\lvertx\rvert)}
  \,,
  \qquad
  x\in\R^{\SpDim}
  \,.
\end{equation}
We assume in this paper, even without further reference, that $u\ge 0$, $u_{0}\ge 0$, $u_{0}\in L^{\infty}(\R^{\SpDim})$ is compactly supported, and that
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:degen}
  1<p<N
  \,,
  \qquad
  p+m-3>0
  \,.
\end{equation}
On $g$ we assume that $g\in C([0,+\infty])\cap C^{1}((0,+\infty))$ is such that $g(0)=0$, $g(s)>0$ for $s>0$, and for given $0<\alpha_{1}\le \alpha_{2}<p$
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:powerlike}
  \alpha_{1}
  \frac{g(s)}{s}
  \le
  g'(s)
  \le
  \alpha_{2}
  \frac{g(s)}{s}
  \,,
  \qquad
  s>0
  \,.
\end{equation}

In \cite{Andreucci:Tedeev:2025} we obtained sup bounds for solutions
to \eqref{eq:pde}--\eqref{eq:initd} in the range $\alpha_{2}<1$, and
estimates of the support for all $\alpha_{2}<Np/(p-1)$. The approach
of \cite{Andreucci:Tedeev:2025} relies on suitable embedding
inequalities and iterative estimates, and is valid for radial or
compactly supported solutions; see also in this connection
\cite{Andreucci:Tedeev:2015} and \cite{Andreucci:Tedeev:2022b}. Here
we present instead new results both for the decay of the solutions in
the $L^{\infty}$ norm and for the finite speed of propagation, by
means of comparison with explicit supersolutions and subsolutions, in
the range $\alpha_{1}$, $\alpha_{2}\in (0,p)$. Our approach is
inspired by the paper \cite{Grillo:Muratori:Vazquez:2017}, where the
authors considered the porous media equation on manifolds. We treat
here the case of the doubly nonlinear equation, and, as a further
novelty even in the case of the porous media equation, we deal
with the case of non-power nonlinearities (satisfying
\eqref{eq:powerlike}). For example the Zygmund function
\begin{equation*}
  g(s)
  =
  s^{\alpha}
  [\log(s+c)]^{\beta}
  \,,
  \quad
  s\ge 0
  \,,
  \alpha>0
  \,,
  \beta>0
  \,,
  c>1
  \,,
\end{equation*}
falls in this class with $\alpha_{1}=\alpha$, $\alpha_{2}=\alpha+\beta$.
\\
We show here that the asymptotic rates found in \cite{Andreucci:Tedeev:2025} are indeed optimal. These bounds are given by
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:sup_decay}
  u(x,t)
  \le
  \gamma
  \Big[
  \frac{g^{(-1)}(\log t)^{p}}{t\log t}
  \Big]^{\frac{1}{p+m-3}}
  \,,
  \quad
  x\in\R^{\SpDim}
  \,,
  t\gg 1
  \,,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:fsp}
  \bar R(t)
  \le
  \gamma
  g^{(-1)}(\log t)
  \,,
  \quad
  t\gg 1
  \,,
\end{equation}
where, for solutions whose support is bounded for $t\ge 0$ we define
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:main_R}
  \bar R(t)
  =
  \inf\{
  r>0
  \mid
  \supp u(t) \subset B_{r}(0)
  \}
  \,.
\end{equation}
Here $\gamma>0$ is a constant depending on $u_{0}$.
According to our present results, the estimates in \eqref{eq:sup_decay}, \eqref{eq:fsp} are indeed sharp, at least for $\alpha_{2}<p$, and, even more, they are extended to such a range for bounded and compactly supported initial data. In addition the comparison results obtained here yield more precise pointwise estimates, tracking the dependence on $\lvertx\rvert$, in comparison to the bounds for the $L^{\infty}(\R^{\SpDim})$ obtained in the quoted papers of the present authors. This is an obvious effect of the barrier function method, which however is in principle less general than the approach through integral inequalities. 

As in \cite{Grillo:Muratori:Vazquez:2017}, we may employ our barrier functions to investigate an equation with inhomogeneous density, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:rad_N}
  \rho(\lvertx\rvert)
  \pder{v}{t}
  =
  \Div(
  v^{m-1}
  \lvert\operatorname{\nabla} v\rvert^{p-2}
  \operatorname{\nabla} v
  )
  \,,
  \qquad
  x\in\numberspacefont{R}^{N}
  \,,
  t>0
  \,.
\end{equation}
For the concept of solutions of \eqref{eq:rad_N}, and other related
information, we refer the reader to \cite{Andreucci:Tedeev:2021b}. In that paper, in fact in a more general setting, we considered equations modeled after \eqref{eq:rad_N}, with, roughly speaking, $\rho(s)$ behaving like $s^{-\alpha}$, $\alpha<p$ for large $s>0$. Here we deal with the new case \eqref{eq:rad_rw_est}, in which essentially $\rho(s)$ behaves like the power $s^{-p}$ multiplied by a powerlike function of $\log s$; this factor may be increasing or decreasing in $s$ according to the values of $\alpha_{1}$, $\alpha_{2}$. We refer to Remark~\ref{r:rad_N} for further comments on this point.

The Cauchy problem for nonlinear, and linear, parabolic equations
involving coefficients strongly depending on the space variable has
received great attention in the literature. We quote
\cite{Grigoryan:2006} which analyzes stochastical completeness and
other qualitative properties of weighted manifolds. In
\cite{Grillo:Muratori:2016} the authors study the smoothness and
temporal decay estimates of the solution to the Cauchy problem for the
porous media equation on Cartan-Hadamard manifolds supporting
Poincar\'{e} inequality. See also \cite{Grillo:Muratori:Vazquez:2017}
for precise two-sides space-time estimates of the solution to the  same problem,
in the setting of
various classes of Cartan-Hadamard manifolds, 
\cite{Muratori:2021} for a survey of results, and again
\cite{Muratori:Roncoroni:2022}
for new Sobolev type inequalities as well as sharp bounds of solutions.
We also quote \cite{Tedeev:2007}, dealing with the influence of an inhomogeneous density on
the interface blow up phenomenon, on finite speed of
propagation, and in general on the 
behavior of  the solution to the Cauchy problem for doubly degenerate
parabolic equations. 
Finally
\cite{Tedeev:2025} investigates the blow up of the solution itself in the same setting.

\subsection{Main results}
\label{s:main}

We define here the spaces $L^{p}_{f}(\R^{\SpDim})$ in a standard way, as the $L^{p}$ spaces relative to the measure $f(x)\di x$.

\begin{definition}
  \label{d:weaksol}
  A function $u\ge 0$ is a weak solution to \eqref{eq:pde} if
  \begin{equation*}
    u\in C([0,+\infty); L^{1}_{f}(\R^{\SpDim})) \cap L^{\infty}(\R^{\SpDim}\times(\bar t,+\infty))
  \end{equation*}
  for all $\bar t>0$, and
  \begin{equation*}
    \operatorname{\nabla} u^{\frac{p+m-2}{p-1}}
    \in
    L^{p}_{\textup{loc}}
    (S_{\infty})
    \,.
  \end{equation*}
  In addition we require the standard integral formulation, i.e., for all $\eta\in C_{0}^{\infty}(S_{\infty})$,
  \begin{equation}
    \label{eq:weaksol_a}
    \int_{0}^{+\infty}
    \int_{\R^{\SpDim}}
    \{
    -
    u
    \eta_{t}
    +
    u^{m-1}
    \lvert\operatorname{\nabla} u\rvert^{p-2}
    \operatorname{\nabla} u
    \operatorname{\nabla} \eta
    \}
    f(x)
    \di x
    \di t
    =
    0
    \,.
  \end{equation}
  \\
  If we also have $u_{t}\in L^{\infty}_{\textup{loc}}(0,+\infty); L^{1}_{f\,\textup{loc}}(\R^{\SpDim}))$, then $u$ is a strong solution.
  \\
  Supersolutions [respectively, subsolutions] to \eqref{eq:pde} are defined similarly, with the difference that, for $\eta\ge0$, we require in \eqref{eq:weaksol_a} an inequality sign $\ge $ [respectively, $\le$].
  \\
  Solutions to the Cauchy problem \eqref{eq:pde}--\eqref{eq:initd} are solutions to \eqref{eq:pde} satisfying $u(0)=u_{0}$ in the sense of $C([0,+\infty); L^{1}_{f}(\R^{\SpDim}))$.
\end{definition}

Existence and comparison results for strong solutions to the problems which we consider here can be proved, at least for sufficiently regular initial data, according for example to the methods of \cite{Degtyarev:Tedeev:2012}, \cite{Tsutsumi:1988}; here we focus rather on estimates of such solutions.

When we consider radial solutions of the form $u(x,t)=U(r,t)$,
$r=\lvertx\rvert$, the equation \eqref{eq:pde} can be written as
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:pde_rad}
  r^{N-1}
  f(r)
  \pder{U}{t}
  =
  \pder{}{r}
  \big[
  r^{N-1}
  f(r)
  U^{m-1}
  \abs{U_{r}}^{p-2}
  U_{r}
  \big]
  \,,
  \qquad
  r>0
  \,,
  t>0
  \,,
\end{equation}
which in turn can be rephrased as
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:sss_rad}
  \begin{split}
    \pder{U}{t}
    &=
      \pder{}{r}
      \big[
      U^{m-1}
      \abs{U_{r}}^{p-2}
      U_{r}
      \big]
    \\
    &\quad+
      \Big(
      \frac{N-1}{r}
      +
      g'
      \Big)
      \big[
      U^{m-1}
      \abs{U_{r}}^{p-2}
      U_{r}
      \big]
      \,,
      \qquad
      r>0
      \,,
      t>0
      \,.
  \end{split}
\end{equation}
Of course the differential inequalities characterizing radial supersolutions and subsolutions can be rephrased similarly.
Specifically, we consider supersolutions and subsolutions of the form
\begin{alignat}2
  \label{eq:sss_sup}
  \widetilde u(x,t)
  &=
    \frac{C_{*}}{(t+t_{0})^{\frac{1}{p+m-3}}}
    \Big[
    E(\tau)^{\frac{1}{p-1}}
    -
    J(r)^{\frac{1}{p-1}}
    \Big]^{\frac{p-1}{p+m-3}}_{+}
    \,,
    &\quad&
            r\ge \rpol_{0}
            \,,
  \\
  \label{eq:sss_ups_2}
  \widetilde u(x,t)
  &=
    \frac{C_{*}}{(t+t_{0})^{\frac{1}{p+m-3}}}
    \Big[
    E(\tau)^{\frac{1}{p-1}}
    -
    I(r)
    \Big]^{\frac{p-1}{p+m-3}}_{+}
    \,,
    &\quad&
            r<\rpol_{0}
            \,,
\end{alignat}
where $r=\lvertx\rvert$, and
\begin{gather*}
  G(r)
  =
  \int_{0}^{r}
  \frac{g(s)}{s}
  \di s
  \,,
  \quad
  r\ge 0
  \,;
  \qquad
  J(r)
  =
  \frac{r^{p}}{G(r)}
  \,,
  \quad
  r>0
  \,.
  \\
  \tau
  =
  \varGamma
  \log(t+t_{0})
  \,,
  \quad
  t\ge 0
  \,;
  \\
  E(\tau)
  =
  J(G^{(-1)}(\tau))
  =
  \frac{G^{(-1)}(\tau)^{p}}{\tau}
  \,,
  \quad
  \tau>0
  \,;
  \\
  I(r)
  =
  \nu_{0}
  \Big(
  \frac{r^{p}}{G(\rpol_{0})}
  \Big)^{\frac{1}{p-1}}
  +
  (1-\nu_{0})
  J(\rpol_{0})^{\frac{1}{p-1}}
  \,,
  \quad
  r>0
  \,.
\end{gather*}
Note that the integral defining $G$ converges according to \eqref{eq:powerlike_lt}.

Here $C_{*}$, $\varGamma$, $t_{0}> 1$, $\rpol_0$ and $\nu_{0}$ are positive constants to be chosen. In particular, $\nu_{0}\in(0,1)$ will be selected so that $\widetilde u$ and $\ups_{r}$ are continuous even at $r=\rpol_{0}$; in fact, clearly, the continuity of $\widetilde u$ holds true for any $\nu_{0}\in(0,1)$.

We also remark that the radius $\tilde r(t)$ of the support of $\widetilde u(t)$ is characterized by $E(\tau)=J(\tilder(t))$, i.e., by $\tilder(t)=G^{(-1)}(\tau)$; according to Lemma~\ref{l:sss_excf} and \eqref{eq:sss_powerlike_gt_excf} this yields
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:sup_sub_radius}
  \gamma_{1}
  g^{(-1)}(\log t)
  \le
  \tilde r(t)
  \le
  \gamma_{2}
  g^{(-1)}(\log t)
  \,,
  \qquad
  t>t_{0}
  \,,
\end{equation}
for two suitable constants $\gamma_{1}$, $\gamma_{2}>0$.

\begin{proposition}
  \label{p:main_sup_sub}
  The constants $C_{*}$, $\varGamma$, $t_{0}> 1$, $\rpol_0$ and
  $\nu_{0}$ can be selected so that $\widetilde u$ as defined in
  \eqref{eq:sss_sup}--\eqref{eq:sss_ups_2} is a strong supersolution to
  \eqref{eq:pde}. Alternatively, we may select such constants so that
  $\widetilde u$ is a strong subsolution to \eqref{eq:pde}.
\end{proposition}

